  "question": {
    "text": "The electric field due to a short electric dipole at a large distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>r</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;from center of dipole on the equatorial plane varies with distance as :"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>r</mi></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>An&nbsp;electric dipole is a system of two charges of equal and opposite magnitude placed at a short distance.</p><p>The dipole moment is,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mi>l</mi></math>.</p><p>The electric field due to the dipole is,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></math></p><p>Clearly, the electric field varies as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></math>.</p></blockquote>"
  }
}